, if we interpret it as the sum of the differences in outcomes across the states (without weighting), then the sum is -1000 (only one non-zero difference). But the reason says 'the more favorable sum of outcome differences'. A negative sum would be less favorable. So the reason would prefer the lottery with a more favorable (i.e., less negative or positive) sum. But both lotteries have the same outcome differences: A has -4000 in 20%, B has -3000 in 25%, 0 in 75%. So in the loss state, A has a larger loss (more negative), so the difference (A - B) is -1000. In the zero state, difference is 0. So the sum of pairwise differences (without weighting) is -1000. But this is the same for both lotteries? No — the sum of differences across states is not the same because the probability of each state differs. If we compute the sum of (A_outcome - B_outcome) across all states, weighted by probabilities, then: P(A_loss) * (-4000 - (-3000)) + P(A_zero) * (0 - 0) = 0.2 * (-1000) + 0.8 * 0 = -200. For lottery B: P(B_loss) * (-3000 - (-4000)) + P(B_zero) * (0 - 0) = 0.25 * (1000) + 0.75 * 0 = 250. So the sum of (A_outcome - B_outcome) weighted by probabilities is -200 for A and +250 for B. So the sum of outcome differences (A - B) is -200 for A, +250 for B. The reason says it prefers the lottery with the more favorable sum of outcome differences. So if we interpret the sum as weighted by probabilities, then the sum of differences (A - B) is -200 for A and +250 for B. So B has a more favorable sum (positive vs negative). Therefore, the reason would prefer B. The information relevant to the decision reason is the weighted sum of outcome differences (A - B) across states, which is -200 for A and +250 for B. Thus, the reason would prefer B.",
